{
  "abstract": "Let $\\chi$ be a real primitive character of conductor $q$. rStation Paper 1 (Appendix C.6) combines Burgess's estimate with Green's determinant to prove $\\liminf L(1,\\chi)\\log q\\ge2\\pi^2/9$, and rStation Paper 58 shows that $2\\pi^2/9$ is exact for these inputs. Here we ask which real zeros $\\sigma=1-\\lambda/\\log q$ the same inputs exclude, through the limiting system of Paper 58. Head mass alone gives $\\lambda>4/3$, the ceiling for transfers through a linear profile (Paper 34). The head shape forced by the cap on the prime density gives $\\lambda\\ge1.50575$. A Blaschke–Krein-type bound for the zeros of $z\\hat\\mu(z)$ gives $\\sum\\operatorname{Re}(-1/\\rho)\\le5/8$, so $\\lambda\\ge8/5$. Adding nonnegativity of the prime density on the whole half-line and the classical $3+4\\cos+\\cos2$ inequality gives $\\lambda\\ge1.60001$. An explicit admissible delay head has its only real root at $1.78967037$, so the infimum $\\lambda_*$ satisfies $1.60001\\le\\lambda_*<1.7896703668$. By compactness of normalised measures, with no upper bound on $L(1,\\chi)\\log q$, $L(\\sigma,\\chi)>0$ for $1-1.60001/\\log q\\le\\sigma<1$ once $q$ is large; this threshold is not computed. Finite-disk versions using only the stated constants of Paper 1 prove the gap $1.59$ when $\\log\\log q\\ge50000$ and $1.60001$ when $\\log\\log q\\ge10^{13}$, with exact rational certificates. The zero-sum bound also gives $L'(1,\\chi)/L(1,\\chi)\\ge-(1/8+o(1))\\log q$, compared with $-1/2$ from the explicit formula, which is Ihara's bound for Euler–Kronecker constants of quadratic fields; we tested the underlying identity on three characters. None of these results is a zero-free region, a new bound for $L(1,\\chi)$ or a class-number result.",
